## 1 Introduction

Neutron stars (NSs) are extremely dense stellar remnants typically observed with masses between 1-2 M_{\\odot} with radii \\sim 12 km (Legred et al., 2021). As such, NSs reach characteristic densities1\\langle\\rho\\rangle=3M/4\\pi R^{3}\\approx 5\\cdot 10^{14} g/cm{}^{3}, roughly twice the density of atomic nuclei (nuclear saturation density: n_{\\rm sat}\\approx 0.16 fm{}^{-3} or \\rho_{\\rm sat}=m_{n}n_{\\rm sat}\\approx 2.8\\times 10^{14} g/cm{}^{3} where m_{n} is the nucleon rest-mass). Therefore, many-body nuclear interactions play a key role in NS structure. At the same time, NSs can reach compactness C=M/R\\sim 1/4, which is close to the upper limit expected from a Schwarzschild Black Hole (BH): C_{\\rm Sch}=1/2. Additionally, the speed of sound (c_{s}) within the NS core almost certainly exceeds 1/\\sqrt{3}(Legred et al., 2021) and therefore reaches an appreciable fraction of the speed of light. Clearly, then, relativistic effects should also be significant.

Footnote 1: I write expressions in units where G=c=1 but provide explicit units when relevant.

The structure of cold, non-rotating NSs is governed by the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff (TOV) equations (Oppenheimer and Volkoff, 1939; Tolman, 1939)2

Footnote 2: It is common practice to solve the TOV equations in a slightly different form, sometimes called the (log)enthalpy formulation (Lindblom, 2014). See also Kastaun and Ohme (2024) for a recent review of numerical techniques. I find that the enthalpy formulation provides a factor of \\sim 5-10 speedup compared to the “standard” formulation described above.

